<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Shuruppak</h1><p class="st-meta">2900 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.) · capital Shuruppak</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sumerian city sacred to the grain goddess Ninlil, remembered as the home of the flood hero and source of some of the oldest literary texts ever found.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Shuruppak, modern Tell Fara, grew on a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in the late fourth millennium BCE and flourished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>. A layer of river sediment dated around 2900 BCE has sometimes been linked to the flood tradition, in which the last king before the deluge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubar-tutu">Ubar-Tutu</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Ziusudra</a> (Utnapishtim) ruled at Shuruppak.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was dedicated to Sud, later identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninlil">Ninlil</a>, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>. Excavations by German and American teams uncovered the Fara archive of about 2600 BCE: land records, lists of workers, lexical lists and some of the earliest literary works. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/instructions-of-shuruppak">Instructions of Shuruppak</a>, proverbial advice attributed to a ruler of the city, is known from copies of about the same period, mostly found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a>. The texts show a large and well-organised household economy, but the names of its rulers are barely known.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Shuruppak came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> after 2334 BCE and later served as a provincial town under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>. It declined soon afterwards and was abandoned by the early second millennium BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shuruppak.jpg" alt="Shuruppak" width="320" height="310"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bill_of_sale_Louvre_AO3765.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2900 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/30th-century-bce">30th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shuruppak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sumerian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fara, Shurubak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2617&amp;focus=shuruppak" aria-label="Shuruppak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5853 1021 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shuruppak lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6173 1277L6129 1232"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6173 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6173 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-2617&amp;focus=shuruppak">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

